1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:05,538
If people from Gefersa to
Dejazmach Yigezu's quarter

2
00:00:05,538 --> 00:00:11,185
asked who the famous chicken
trader of Gulele was, they

3
00:00:11,185 --> 00:00:16,831
were told: Tebeje. Tebeje the
drunk. Tebeje the chicken

4
00:00:16,831 --> 00:00:17,550
trader.

5
00:00:18,033 --> 00:00:23,253
Whether he knew them or not,
at night he greeted everyone.

6
00:00:23,253 --> 00:00:28,023
Whether he knew them or not,
at night he laughed with

7
00:00:28,023 --> 00:00:28,833
everyone.

8
00:00:29,325 --> 00:00:34,786
When coffee was brewed by
every hearth in Gulele, every

9
00:00:34,786 --> 00:00:40,247
drunkard's tale anyone told
was told about Tebeje. Even

10
00:00:40,247 --> 00:00:45,807
old widows who had never seen
his face sipped from their

11
00:00:45,807 --> 00:00:50,275
cups and pinned other drunks'
stories on him.

12
00:00:50,783 --> 00:00:58,230
This was true. Whether worse
than they said or better, he

13
00:00:58,230 --> 00:01:04,371
came out of Gulele's
eucalyptus and appeared to

14
00:01:04,371 --> 00:01:08,683
Addis Ababa as a strange new
man.

15
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:15,604
By the old timber customs,
heading east, across the

16
00:01:15,604 --> 00:01:22,510
bridge over the river, the
first small thatched hut you

17
00:01:22,510 --> 00:01:29,415
reach: that is Tebeje's house.
There, in that house, in

18
00:01:29,415 --> 00:01:31,550
Gulele, he lived.

19
00:01:32,033 --> 00:01:37,558
He carried the timber and dug
the earth; he built the house

20
00:01:37,558 --> 00:01:42,333
himself and paid for the
thatch. Tebeje felt proud.

21
00:01:42,825 --> 00:01:48,025
Then his eyes filled with
tears. A tear ran down his

22
00:01:48,025 --> 00:01:53,625
cheek, and he crushed it with
the palm of his left hand.

23
00:01:54,117 --> 00:02:00,564
'Which chicken trader?' 'That
drunkard of Gulele.' 'Oh! Me,

24
00:02:00,564 --> 00:02:04,717
Tebeje, I am ruined,' he said,
shaken.

25
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:09,892
'From now on, for the honour
of this house at least, I must

26
00:02:09,892 --> 00:02:11,800
quit drinking,' he said.

27
00:02:12,283 --> 00:02:16,028
'Gulele is asleep. If I sit
under this eucalyptus and

28
00:02:16,028 --> 00:02:18,783
drink, no one will see me,' he
thought.

29
00:02:19,283 --> 00:02:22,892
'I've slurped about half of
it,' he said, and burst out

30
00:02:22,892 --> 00:02:23,483
laughing.

31
00:02:23,992 --> 00:02:28,720
'Where did you last see the
maid?' he asked, and began

32
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:31,522
diving and swimming to find
her.

33
00:02:32,033 --> 00:02:36,627
'Is the woman saved?' he asked
tenderly. One came closer:

34
00:02:36,627 --> 00:02:38,883
'It's a pig you pulled out.'

35
00:02:39,367 --> 00:02:45,685
All his good, brave deeds were
counted cheap, because he was

36
00:02:45,685 --> 00:02:48,317
known to be always drunk.

37
00:02:48,825 --> 00:02:55,012
From five months after he
built the house, Tebeje never

38
00:02:55,012 --> 00:02:56,475
drank at all.

39
00:02:56,992 --> 00:03:02,492
'If I slip out, just today,
just a little, just once, just

40
00:03:02,492 --> 00:03:05,242
me... what harm could it do?'

41
00:03:05,742 --> 00:03:08,857
Like a beloved leader they
carried him on their

42
00:03:08,857 --> 00:03:11,442
shoulders, passing him along,
cheering.

43
00:03:11,950 --> 00:03:19,837
A great force struck him from
behind. A fox hole caught him

44
00:03:19,837 --> 00:03:25,050
in front. His leg shattered
like glass.

45
00:03:25,533 --> 00:03:31,462
But another said, 'So he never
drinks again, I'll cut it off

46
00:03:31,462 --> 00:03:33,833
at the hip,' and he did.

47
00:03:34,325 --> 00:03:40,169
Just then the dogs fighting
over a bone tumbled onto him,

48
00:03:40,169 --> 00:03:42,425
and he woke in terror.

49
00:03:42,908 --> 00:03:47,544
'How many legs do I have?' he
asked. Weyzero Tiruneshe

50
00:03:47,544 --> 00:03:51,408
replied, 'How many did you
have before, sir?'

51
00:03:51,908 --> 00:03:57,925
'Truly, you have two legs,
sir,' she said. Tebeje bent to

52
00:03:57,925 --> 00:04:01,408
kiss his leg, but it was too
far.

53
00:04:01,908 --> 00:04:08,596
Looking at the sky he said,
'For me, drink means death.'

54
00:04:08,596 --> 00:04:15,643
It was night. 'Nights of such
vows pass,' Weyzero Tiruneshe

55
00:04:15,643 --> 00:04:16,598
replied.

